WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:13.920
Hello everyone, I am very excited to be here
for a panel that I hope will be interesting,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.029
provocative, thought provoking, and we have
some amazing panelists for you today.

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:25.769
So our first participant is an associate professor
of psychology and neuroscience at New York

00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:30.400
University and he conducts research on how
group identities, moral values and political

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.210
beliefs shape the mind and brain.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.010
Please welcome Jay Van Bavel.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:43.600
Our next participant is a clinical psychologist
at the Bellevue NYU Program for Survivors

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.440
of Torture where she provides clinical care
to individuals and families who have survived

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.710
torture and refugee trauma.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:52.899
Please welcome Katherine Porterfield.

00:00:52.899 --> 00:01:00.710
And our final guest today is a social psychologist
also at New York University's Stern School

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:06.750
of Business, whose research examines the intuitive
foundations of morality and how morality varies

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.300
across cultural and political divides.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.360
Please welcome Jonathan Haidt.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.380
Thank you guys all for being here today.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:22.460
Um, I think we are in for a fascinating conversation
and I wanted to begin by kind of setting the

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.390
parameters of the discussion.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:30.540
So in your mind and I'd like all three of
you to address this, what is extremism?

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:35.320
You know, what, when you say extremism, when
you think of extremism in your work, what

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.320
does it mean to you?

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.320
Maybe we can start with Jay.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.320
Yeah.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.100
I would just say it means to me that someone
has a very rigid dogmatic belief system and

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.360
they've started to see the world in black
and white and lost any sense of shades of

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.360
gray.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.360
Okay.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.360
Kate?’

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.270
Yeah, I mean I think we probably all anchor
to our own thing that we do in the world.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:59.250
And so I work a lot with people who have been
subject to extremist acts or themselves have

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.020
attempted to or done extremist acts.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:08.610
So I think of it as sort of enacting or thinking
about enacting behavior that reflects very

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.729
rigid black and white viewpoints.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:16.700
And I do tend to usually think it that veers
towards ideas about violence or at least oppressing

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.900
the rights of others.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.350
Um, so as, as a social psychologist my, my
test for what social psychology studies I

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:28.790
like to start with homo economicus, is imagine
a person who only does things that are in

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:35.090
his or her self interest and then everything
about what we people actually do that is beyond

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:40.000
that, that social psychology and to the extent
that sometimes we do things that seem possibly

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.080
incredibly self destructive.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:48.740
People are willing to face arrest, people
are willing to face violence.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.340
People are willing to sometimes even blow
themselves up.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:57.240
Those are acts so far beyond our self interest,
we tend to label those as extremisms.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.670
Just building onto Jay's definition.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:03.350
I would just caution that we can't identify
just the patterns of thought because we all

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:04.820
feel passionate about certain things.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:07.400
So extremism to some extent is what your enemies
do.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.300
Where we ourselves are never extremists.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:12.500
I think that's.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.690
I think that's a really interesting point.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:19.050
Jay, would would your work actually back some
of that up because I know you've done some

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:22.340
work on what we ourselves see ourselves as,
versus...

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:27.980
So to Jon's point, I have a paper on the psychology
of hate, which there's a lot of different

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.980
theories floating around that are several
thousands of years old.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.290
They back to Aristotle and very little research
on the psychology of hate in part because

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.890
it's hard to put it in a bottle and study
it in the lab.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:42.290
And so one thing we did is we ran some experiments
trying to figure out how people think about

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:47.740
hate differently from other forms of dislike
that are extreme and it seems like it's more

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:48.880
negative for sure.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.610
There's a difference of degree, but there's
also a difference of kind.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.310
And the additional ingredient, Jon, is morality.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:59.090
And so to take that to the real world, and
this might address where Kate's interests

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:04.880
are, we analyze the content on hate websites
from 50 different hate groups.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.080
And those of you on the panel might know this.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.880
Some of the very first groups to take advantage
of the Internet were hate groups.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:17.930
And so if you look at the content of their
language on their websites, it is moralized.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:21.739
That's one thing that makes it truly different
from other types of complaint forums or negative

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:25.810
types of websites and if you actually read
it carefully, it's not just that it has moral

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:26.810
content.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:30.539
They see themselves as virtuous defenders
of some set of values and so these are the

00:04:30.539 --> 00:04:35.349
people that we think of as the most reprehensible
members of our society, but they certainly

00:04:35.349 --> 00:04:37.919
don't see themselves that way.

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:42.479
So one of my favorite metaphors for understanding
weird things in society is the headline.

00:04:42.479 --> 00:04:49.169
I think it actually appeared in a British
newspaper: fog in channel continent cut off.

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:52.800
And you know, because obviously Britain to
say that the continent was cut off that's

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.830
looking at it from their perspective.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:55.830
Okay.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:59.699
Maybe you didn't find it as funny as I did,
but it's a good metaphor for a lot of things

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:05.860
because here we are in this extraordinary
advanced western secular civilization, which

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:11.059
you know, really just sort of came up in the
last 100, 200 years, however you want to count

00:05:11.059 --> 00:05:14.849
it, and we're talking about what those extremists
do when actually I think the better way to

00:05:14.849 --> 00:05:19.770
look at is to flip the lens around and say,
as a species, human beings, for as long as

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:24.919
we have an anthro archaeological record, human
beings have painted their skins, danced around

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:30.469
a campfire, worshipped various animate objects,
engaged in violent intergroup competition,

00:05:30.469 --> 00:05:33.839
and lived a very, very intense ritual groupish
life.

00:05:33.839 --> 00:05:34.889
That's what we do.

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:35.889
That's who we are.

00:05:35.889 --> 00:05:37.569
Now, what we need to explain is why did that
stop?

00:05:37.569 --> 00:05:41.370
Why did that stop for us, for our cultural
ancestors?

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:45.159
And there's a wonderful book by Barbara Ehrenreich,
the woman who wrote “Nickel and Dimed”.

00:05:45.159 --> 00:05:48.620
She has this fantastic book I don't hear people
talk about called “Dancing In the Streets”,

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:54.069
A history of collective joy, and she starts
from reading all the explorer, like the European

00:05:54.069 --> 00:05:55.069
explorers.

00:05:55.069 --> 00:05:59.000
When they went out all around the world, despite
all the variety of civilizations, they went

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.430
to, pretty much everyone everywhere.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.610
They danced around the campfire to rhythmic
music.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:07.930
They had all kinds of rites and rituals and
this stuff started a lot of the great sociologists

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:12.749
like Durkheim and Weber and others, this is
what we do and then for some reason and a

00:06:12.749 --> 00:06:16.449
lot of it has to do with an advanced commercial
society in which we're all focused on making

00:06:16.449 --> 00:06:17.699
money and trade.

00:06:17.699 --> 00:06:22.680
That dampens down the extremism you might
say, that makes us secular creatures more

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:27.919
homo economicus is really focused on advancing
our self interests and so sometimes we lose

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:32.319
that and we go back to our normal extremist
and that's the way I would reframe this.

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:35.089
So almost right now going back to some of
our roots.

00:06:35.089 --> 00:06:36.400
Yes, absolutely.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:37.400
Yeah.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:43.200
And so this actually makes me wonder kind
of if we look at extremism over time, not

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.270
just kind of ebbs and flows, but also the
definitions of what is and isn't seen as extreme.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:54.869
Is there also kind of a component of that
that as society evolves that we actually start

00:06:54.869 --> 00:06:56.659
seeing different behaviors as extreme?

00:06:56.659 --> 00:07:01.449
Or is it something that's always, you know,
this has always been extreme in any society.

00:07:01.449 --> 00:07:06.580
Um, I think that some of it might be a little
bit more context determined?

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.550
What would you guys say about that?

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:14.649
Yeah, I would say that culture and context
play a huge role in what we define as appropriate

00:07:14.649 --> 00:07:15.849
or inappropriate.

00:07:15.849 --> 00:07:21.310
So one example in our lifetimes, a moral issue
that's changed a lot, is attitudes towards

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:28.770
homosexuality, same sex marriage, gay rights,
LGBT rights in the last decade.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.719
They've completely reversed in terms of approval
versus disapproval.

00:07:31.719 --> 00:07:38.939
And so what was objectionable in fact, illegal
not that long ago is legal and celebrated

00:07:38.939 --> 00:07:43.319
and New York's going to be having a huge pride
parade in the near future.

00:07:43.319 --> 00:07:50.610
And so what was considered an extreme event,
so it's always a, it's a minority of the population

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.740
who is gay.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:57.309
So it's always been outside of the majority,
but what, how we've defined it and acceptance

00:07:57.309 --> 00:08:00.059
of those types of behaviors has changed dramatically.

00:08:00.059 --> 00:08:04.349
And this is something that is a highly moralized,
uh, has historically for a lot of people and

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:09.559
so things change pretty quickly about what
is illegal behavior and unethical behavior

00:08:09.559 --> 00:08:14.679
or immoral behavior to something that is accepted
and embraced and celebrated.

00:08:14.679 --> 00:08:19.210
I was just going to say that the flip side
of it unfortunately too though, is that like

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:26.830
if you look after 9/11 where sort of normal
practice of Islam became very frequently labeled

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:32.440
extremist by a lot of regular Americans who
got scared and reacted to a lot of the rhetoric.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:36.990
And so the flip side I think is that what's
called extremist now is actually sometimes

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:42.789
of pathologizing of normal, you know, faith
based practice or, or you know, wearing hijab

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:44.070
or you know, whatever.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:47.899
And so now that's been labeled extremist whereas,
you know, you sort of look at white supremacy,

00:08:47.899 --> 00:08:49.750
which has always been, always been.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:52.240
It's always been that white supremacists have
been looked at as extremists.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:53.240
But.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:54.850
But you do.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:55.850
Yeah?

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:56.850
Well...ok always.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:00.769
The Klan was not an extremist organization
when it was founded.

00:09:00.769 --> 00:09:01.769
It was, but...

00:09:01.769 --> 00:09:02.769
No, no, no, I, I, good point.

00:09:02.769 --> 00:09:06.740
I sort of meant in the last, like maybe I
was reaching...

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:08.529
...In our lives...

00:09:08.529 --> 00:09:14.029
Even that's probably...anyway, but you look
at last year where then sort of that group's

00:09:14.029 --> 00:09:19.250
behavior got normalized a bit and less and
sort of viewed less as extremist and brought

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:20.250
into the mainstream.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:26.410
So it's, it's definitely culturally, um, what's
the word sort of normed by what cultural responses

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:27.410
are.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:33.090
So I think just the very word extremist in
the word itself, it tells us that it's a statistical

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:34.090
term.

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:36.980
It's so, it's just relative to what most people
are doing, that's extremist.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:40.270
So I think we need to keep in mind that by
definition there's always extremes.

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:44.470
And then we can also as a separate or related
conversation, we can talk about the willingness

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:48.019
to use violence and force to get your way.

00:09:48.019 --> 00:09:51.779
That is something that can be defined more
objectively, in some societies that's the

00:09:51.779 --> 00:09:53.420
norm.

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:57.209
And in American society, for the in the post
war years, that was very much not the norm.

00:09:57.209 --> 00:10:00.940
We had a burst of it in the sixties and we're
getting a little bit more of it in the last

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:01.950
couple of years on campus.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:07.440
Not a lot, but so, so I would urge us to maybe
even focus on that because that's really what's

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.740
a problem for society.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:12.700
It's not that there are people far away, it's
that there are people willing to use violence

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:14.720
and force to get their way.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:16.649
And do you think that that's.

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:21.470
Do you think that we need to differentiate
between kind of that definition in different

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:22.470
domains?

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:27.530
So like, you know, we have obviously religious
extremism, but there's also political extremism

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:28.620
which you just alluded to.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.769
There's cultural extremism and you know, we
have all of these different kind of domain

00:10:32.769 --> 00:10:38.341
specific areas and in some of them you might
say you have very negative kind of pernicious

00:10:38.341 --> 00:10:40.190
social effects without any violence.

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:44.089
Like if the extremism is something about First
Amendment for instance.

00:10:44.089 --> 00:10:47.899
Um, so that's, that can be extremism, but
without violence.

00:10:47.899 --> 00:10:51.410
So do we, are we talking, does it always have
to be physical violence?

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.500
Does it depend on domain?

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.920
You know, is there differences, you know,
in, in the work that you've done with these

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:00.410
sorts of different types of extremism?

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:04.550
I'm just curious to hear all of your, all
of your thoughts on, on kind of that.

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:06.970
Well, I tend to focus on process.

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:11.660
I think the miracle of our modern secular
societies is that we develop processes.

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:15.410
I often say, thank God we were settled by
the British rather than the Spanish.

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:17.060
My friends in Latin America say that to me
too.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:18.399
If only we were settled by the British.

00:11:18.399 --> 00:11:20.170
The British give us really good institutions.

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:25.270
The Spanish had colonial institutions of extraction,
terrible for setting up a secular liberal

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:26.279
democracy.

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:30.319
So the British did a fantastic job giving
institutions, you see this in many former

00:11:30.319 --> 00:11:32.129
British colonies.

00:11:32.129 --> 00:11:37.519
But then sometimes those process, the willingness
to abide by process and rule of law, sometimes

00:11:37.519 --> 00:11:42.889
people violate that because that's not really
who we are as a species or basic nature is

00:11:42.889 --> 00:11:48.279
much more prone to to saying the ends justify
the means and if I'm sure that my side is

00:11:48.279 --> 00:11:52.029
right and the courts have ruled that I'm wrong
to hell with the courts.

00:11:52.029 --> 00:11:57.709
That's normal human psychology and if we have
certain parameters, we end up following the

00:11:57.709 --> 00:11:58.709
rules.

00:11:58.709 --> 00:12:02.069
We have procedural justice, we don't have
violent extremism and what's breaking down

00:12:02.069 --> 00:12:08.579
now, that we have fallen off a cliff, support
for democratic norms and faith in democracy.

00:12:08.579 --> 00:12:13.379
Falling off a cliff with each new generation
is that this can be a lot less willingness

00:12:13.379 --> 00:12:14.459
to respect democratic process.

00:12:14.459 --> 00:12:17.329
It's going to get a lot worse in the next
couple of decades is my prediction.

00:12:17.329 --> 00:12:21.199
I guess I feel like the only place I'd sort
of counter that a bit though is in my experience

00:12:21.199 --> 00:12:26.139
working with people who really have gone over
to the fringe of being willing to enact violence.

00:12:26.139 --> 00:12:30.850
So whether, you know, planning an attack or
engaging in something or trying to fly to

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:38.089
a place where they could do that, I guess
to, to a person, these have all been people

00:12:38.089 --> 00:12:42.510
who've suffered really extreme trauma and
marginalization in their lives.

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:50.620
And so systems are not something that they
were raised to experience as beneficent, life

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:52.459
giving, nurturing, you know, fill in the blank.

00:12:52.459 --> 00:12:56.810
So to them the system is a joke and it's not
unlike, I think what you see sometimes in

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:00.009
gang membership, which is another kind of
extremism we haven't really talked about.

00:13:00.009 --> 00:13:05.269
But so the idea, I think there's a lot of
people who when they get raised in, you know,

00:13:05.269 --> 00:13:10.470
good enough home with enough resources and
a decent enough community in school, they

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:14.150
follow the rules and they you know they abide
by stuff and they believe in the system.

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:15.150
You know what I mean?

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:19.790
When I get a traffic ticket I get annoyed,
but I usually pay it or if I dispute it and

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:21.310
I lose, I'm annoyed even more.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:23.209
But I still say, okay, well I lost.

00:13:23.209 --> 00:13:26.939
In other words, I accept that that is the
system and that's probably because I had a

00:13:26.939 --> 00:13:29.290
decent enough life growing up that I believe
in systems.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:30.290
You see what I mean?

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:34.220
No I do and I'm sure that there are, there
are people that may be, especially with white

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:37.199
supremacy groups, there are people like there's
a guy who's Picciolini?

00:13:37.199 --> 00:13:42.199
There's a guy who has a book now out now,
he was a neo Nazi and he left, but there certainly

00:13:42.199 --> 00:13:47.279
are ways in which people are sort of part
of the underclass under culture, on the margins

00:13:47.279 --> 00:13:51.139
legally, drugs and petty crime and then they
move on to other things.

00:13:51.139 --> 00:13:52.959
So that would be the process you're talking
about.

00:13:52.959 --> 00:13:56.830
But there's been a huge amount of research
on terrorist org, like formal organized terrorist

00:13:56.830 --> 00:14:00.750
organizations that generally finds they're
educated middle class people.

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:04.749
So there's a lot of psychological research
on the Baader-Meinhof Gang in Germany in west

00:14:04.749 --> 00:14:05.749
Germany.

00:14:05.749 --> 00:14:07.860
A lot of research on the background of 9/11
terrorists.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.260
These were middle class college educated people.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:15.250
There was no psychopathology and so the general
route that social psychologists have taken

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:18.930
here, I'm talking about especially a Clark
McCauley and Scott Atran are two of the ones

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.279
that I know, I know their work.

00:14:20.279 --> 00:14:24.400
What they both came to the conclusion is this
has nothing to do with psychopathology.

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:28.569
These are people who get in because of small
groups and attachment to groups.

00:14:28.569 --> 00:14:34.040
It's, it's a, it's a real...Atran's work shows
that especially the Islamic terrorists, or

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:38.100
or Al Qaeda in particular recruited through
soccer groups in Europe.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:43.550
So if you have immigrants from Muslim country
in Spain, they'll be in a soccer league and

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:44.550
it's a team.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:45.550
It's the men of a team.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:49.040
They're the ones who get radicalized by one
guy and before you know it, they're going

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:50.740
to different places for training.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:53.589
So it's, you have to look at the small groups,
I would say as a social psychologist.

00:14:53.589 --> 00:14:54.589
Yeah.

00:14:54.589 --> 00:14:57.149
And Jay, some of your work actually talks
about that.

00:14:57.149 --> 00:14:58.220
Do you want to address this point?

00:14:58.220 --> 00:14:59.220
Yeah.

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:04.470
So I'm nodding with from Jon's perspective
in that group identity and the psychology

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:10.110
behind that is I think like the first ingredient
when I think of these types of issues in society

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:14.889
and then I think the second ingredient is
maybe competition with another group or the

00:15:14.889 --> 00:15:15.889
clear enemy.

00:15:15.889 --> 00:15:17.709
So then you get an us versus them and then.

00:15:17.709 --> 00:15:22.189
The third ingredient and this cuts to Atran's
work and yours is morality.

00:15:22.189 --> 00:15:28.639
Once it becomes a sacred domain and you're
fighting over land or some ideology, then

00:15:28.639 --> 00:15:34.370
you have the kind of, to me the three ingredients
that lead to people being willing to engage

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:35.439
in violent behavior.

00:15:35.439 --> 00:15:37.879
So if you want to anchor on that definition.

00:15:37.879 --> 00:15:43.540
I think you got to put in there though...I'm
always the clinical psychologist, I'm always

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:48.480
going to like, to the unit of one, which is
for me, there's always sense of self, like

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:52.800
you know, and again I have a small and I haven't
researched stuff, but you know, I've, I've

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:57.740
seen a fair number of people who've done things
or tried to do things and you know, for me

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:04.220
the pathology as it were, has usually rested
in sense of self and sense of meaning making

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:06.649
about other people.

00:16:06.649 --> 00:16:11.230
Let me push on that and I think that maybe
there's a way for us to blend our ideas together.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:13.949
So this also connects to Atran's work with
terrorists.

00:16:13.949 --> 00:16:19.249
If you measure their overlap with their group,
so some people have a very distinct sense

00:16:19.249 --> 00:16:23.959
of self that's different from the groups they
belong to and so they're non overlapping circles.

00:16:23.959 --> 00:16:27.790
People in terrorist groups from his research
anyways finds that those circles are perfectly

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:30.209
overlapping, well, he calls it identity fusion.

00:16:30.209 --> 00:16:32.279
Their sense of self is the group.

00:16:32.279 --> 00:16:34.270
And that's why it's easy to engage in things
like self-sacrifice.

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:35.270
Yeah.

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:36.569
I'd like to build on that.

00:16:36.569 --> 00:16:38.180
The idea of identity fusion.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:42.259
So it may start with a construct from an individual
pathology, could start from a small group,

00:16:42.259 --> 00:16:46.240
but what you often get when people work together
is you get a complete loss of the sense of

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:47.240
self.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:53.019
And what really clued me into this is a wonderful
book I read by a man named William McNeill

00:16:53.019 --> 00:16:55.730
who wrote a book called “Keeping Together
in Time”.

00:16:55.730 --> 00:17:00.339
And it starts where he, he goes to basic training
in Texas.

00:17:00.339 --> 00:17:02.759
This is 1940 or 41 just before the war starts.

00:17:02.759 --> 00:17:06.230
The US is gearing up and it's very under equipped.

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:09.920
They don't have real guns, so they just march
up and down all day in the hot Texas sun with

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:11.740
sticks and it seems stupid.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:15.860
But by two or three days of marching, he has
these self transcending experiences and he

00:17:15.860 --> 00:17:18.410
describes what is it like when the men get
it?

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:20.680
When you are moving as a unit, it's thrilling.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:22.190
You lose yourself.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:23.860
And so he wrote, he quotes all these other
books.

00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:28.290
I read all these other wonderful books and
I took a quote from one of them that really

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:34.120
describes the loss of self that men feel in
battle, and that is a...anyway, we have a

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:35.120
video.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.090
I hired some people to make a video, we have
like a 90 second video.

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:41.410
This is from a book by Jesse Glenn Gray called
Men and War, I think it was.

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:47.530
Anyway, so this is a quote from Gray describing
interviews he did with men in World War Two.

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:52.750
[Video] Many veterans will admit that the
experience of communal effort in battle has

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:55.900
been the high point of their lives.

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:59.080
I passes insensibly into a we.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:03.680
My becomes our, an individual fate loses its
central importance.

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:12.400
I believe that it is nothing less than the
assurance of immortality that makes self sacrifice

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:14.930
at these moments so relatively easy.

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:23.930
I may fall, but I do not die, for that which
is real in me, goes forward and lives on in

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:26.860
the comrades for whom I gave up my life.

00:18:26.860 --> 00:18:31.060
So the, uh, the conclusion I came to in writing
my book, “The Righteous Mind”, is that

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:36.810
human beings are products of multilevel selection,
which is probably setting off alarms in some

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:41.310
vi who read Richard Dawkins and believed that,
Oh multilevel selection, that's a heresy that's

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:42.310
totally wrong.

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:45.540
Our human nature is overwhelmingly the product
of individual level selection.

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:49.200
Our ancestors were good at competing with
other people in their group.

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:50.200
That's individual level selection.

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:53.680
It makes us largely selfish, we're generous
to the extent that it ends up helping us in

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:54.680
the long run.

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:59.470
But, but I believe we have a period of several
hundred thousand years in human evolution

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:02.770
from anywhere from a million years ago to
actually the present in which there was a

00:19:02.770 --> 00:19:07.810
lot of group versus group violence or just
competition and all of us in this room are

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:11.900
descended from the groups that didn't get
wiped out by other groups.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:17.920
The groups that had combinations of genetic
and especially cultural adaptations, especially

00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:22.580
religion that bound them together, made them
work as a unit, made them fierce in war, made

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:25.530
them able to cooperate to in bad times.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:30.860
Um, so I believe that a phrase I use in the
book is that human beings are 90 percent chimp

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:32.620
and 10 percent bee.

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:36.720
It's not that our ancestors had sex with a
bee, it's that they went through a little

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:41.180
bit of a process of group level selection
which gave us this tribal or groupish overlay.

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:43.170
And that's what I was trying to illustrate
in that video.

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:48.150
Um, and Jay, you've actually done some work
on tribalism and kind of what, what happens

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:49.150
and how that develops.

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.020
Do you want to talk a little about your research?

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:54.670
So I'll, I'll say this is one of the things
that fascinates me.

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:58.540
And Jon, I think you called this the hive
switch.

00:19:58.540 --> 00:20:02.750
And so there's classic research in psychology
that I've followed up on where basically you

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:03.750
flip a coin.

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:07.510
If I was a flip a coin and decide that this
group is going to be in one group and this

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:09.360
side of the room is gonna be another group.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:12.280
So that's gonna be the red team and the blue
team.

00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:16.930
We can ask each other by the end of the session
how much you like and identify through group

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:17.930
versus other group.

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:21.890
And I do this in my classes all the time and
just without exception, people start to say

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:23.211
they like members of their own group more.

00:20:23.211 --> 00:20:25.200
Even if they don't interact with them.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:28.031
In these studies, they find that they like
their own group members more even if they

00:20:28.031 --> 00:20:33.720
don't see them or know who they are and humans
are distinct among primates in that we will

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:38.530
be prosocial and cooperate with in group members
who are anonymous, we've never met.

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:40.300
No other primate will do that.

00:20:40.300 --> 00:20:46.910
And so there's something about the human mind
that allows us to shift into this sense of

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:49.430
identity and draw these boundaries with other
groups.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:55.410
And what we found is in the scanner, so we
bring people in and measured their brain activation

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:59.910
and you start to see differences emerge within
a few minutes after you do this and people's

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:05.770
brains even very low level automatic responses
in their brain, in their amygdala, which is

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:09.730
basically detecting things that are important,
start to shift from ways of chunking up the

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:14.520
world that we're used to like black and white
to ingroup and outgroup on the basis of how

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:15.950
we flipped a coin.

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:19.810
The other thing we found in a recent paper
is the same patterns of activation in the

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:24.890
brain when you flip this coin and create these
groups, predict how you're seeing the world

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:25.890
politically.

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:29.310
So when we think of Democrats and Republicans,
we think, well, they have very different policies

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:32.930
and platforms and we've thought about the
world in several decades thinking about our

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:39.800
politics and certainly that plays a role,
but there's also this foundation of groupishness

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:43.130
and that the same patterns of the brain that
respond to red team and blue team based on

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:47.650
a coin flip are active when you're thinking
of red team and blue team in terms of Democrats

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:48.650
and Republicans.

00:21:48.650 --> 00:21:56.280
And so kind of a core root of political divides
and divides and all kinds of other groupish

00:21:56.280 --> 00:22:02.500
domains, nationality, soccer teams, religious
differences have, are grounded in this kind

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:04.090
of basic psychology.

00:22:04.090 --> 00:22:10.170
The willingness to do violence part of the
group psychology becomes for me, um, it just,

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:14.910
it often I think roots into individual development
through childhood.

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:18.430
And so for instance, if we divide the group
up and then said, okay, now start fighting

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:22.380
each other, probably a couple of you would
refrain, right?

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:25.260
Most of you wouldn't do violence because of
this group, but.

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:31.650
So the willingness to do that for me starts
going back to that developmental experience

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:35.830
that that kid had, you know, what was, what,
what did other people mean to that kid, you

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:39.330
know, were they a source of fear were they
a source of degradation and humiliation because

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:43.560
then what you're starting to do is wire in
to that kid's brain a sense that other people

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:46.720
are dangerous and other people are a source
of humiliation.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:51.520
And then meanings about that are much easier
attached onto that kid, is sort of how I think

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:54.350
of the kids I've seen who've gotten into extreme
stuff.

00:22:54.350 --> 00:22:58.460
It's like, you know, as they start reading,
it's like barnacles that can just attach onto

00:22:58.460 --> 00:22:59.460
them easier.

00:22:59.460 --> 00:23:03.330
You know, I've got teenagers who have all
kinds of crazy ideas, but I don't think you

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:08.750
could convince them that they should go out
and kill people because I hope, you know,

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:13.970
they've had a good enough upbringing that
that doesn't attach to meanings that they

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:18.090
feel in their body and in their mind from
having been degraded, been marginalized, been

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:19.090
humiliated.

00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:22.730
So I just feel like I always have to pull
towards that individual psychology part, you

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:23.730
know.

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:25.280
I'll defer to Jon and then I have a comment
about that as well.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:26.280
Okay.

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:28.930
So the question is, given what you just said
about red team, blue team and how quickly

00:23:28.930 --> 00:23:31.080
we divide up this minimal groups research.

00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:35.150
I'd like to ask your opinion of what we're
doing in high schools and colleges as we teach

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:39.880
kids identity identity politics, how we encourage
them.

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:45.600
For much of the 20th century, we're headed
towards a regime of minimizing racial identity

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:50.540
and maximizing the sense of shared identity
and trying to get beyond racial divisions

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:54.750
and the last few years there's been a huge
surge in programming and efforts to directly

00:23:54.750 --> 00:24:02.020
teach various theories and it's a lot of initiate,
a lot of orientation in universities now and

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:06.990
a lot of high school programs are encouraging
kids to be more identified and to see each

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:09.450
other in terms of their category memberships.

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:11.260
What do you think of this?

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:15.390
So I don't know the specifics of this program
and, and this is going to be a whole minefield

00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:16.570
I'll walk into now...

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:19.160
Never been happier that I work in prisons
by the way.

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:22.040
I can be like no idea,

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:23.040
Okay.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:27.450
So I'm not familiar with how this is happening
in high school, so I'm just going to express

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:29.160
ignorance about that.

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:34.080
What I would say is this, so I've studied
this many times in the context of race.

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:39.460
If you create mixed race teams, people start
to show positive attitudes to whoever's in

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:41.260
their team, whether they're black or white.

00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:44.720
And so that's a positive benefit of these
superordinate identities.

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:48.770
And that's been shown over and over again
by many other labs and research teams other

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:49.990
than than my own.

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:54.730
So that's a robust finding and and a really
positive element of groupishness and identity

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:56.000
that we could.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:59.890
It has this dark side, but also has a bright
side that we can come together to accomplish

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:00.890
it.

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:01.890
That's right.

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:05.190
The more we emphasize superordinate identities
and common goals, the more you can get past

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:07.050
any kind of race and bigotry.

00:25:07.050 --> 00:25:08.050
Yeah.

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:12.380
But then there's one qualification of that
in the literature, which is that when minority

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:17.730
groups are expected to subsume into the majority
group, that can cause tension because they

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:21.760
feel like they're having to adopt all the
values of the majority group.

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:27.250
And so it turns out it works best for them
if they can retain their identity and celebrate

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:28.250
their identity.

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:32.510
So this is, I'm Canadian and so this is part
of multiculturalism in Canada, which is that

00:25:32.510 --> 00:25:36.390
you have a broader Canadian identity when
the Olympics are on everybody's cheering for

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:37.710
the Canadian hockey teams.

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:43.000
But at the same time that you celebrate the
subordinate identities that you have and that

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:47.610
we celebrate difference and so if you can,
I think that it works best, there's a couple

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:50.750
labs who have found this, it works best if
you can do both well.

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:54.840
But I think if you're just focusing on the
lower order identities without nourishing

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:58.710
the higher order one, that would be a direction
I wouldn't recommend.

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:00.040
That's right, and that's the direction we're
heading.

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:02.810
That's one of the reasons I think things are
going to get a lot worse in this country.

00:26:02.810 --> 00:26:06.330
Yeah, but I think if you can do both and if
the leaders in the country can promote both

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:09.950
than I think that that the research would
suggest that's a better pathway to success.

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:10.950
I agree.

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:12.220
Well, it's not unlike what we do when.

00:26:12.220 --> 00:26:15.800
I mean, when you have a kid in front of you,
when, when I do that, I'm working with who's

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:20.420
thought about going and joining an extreme
organization and doing something violent.

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:26.010
I mean, the way I work usually with a team
towards helping him or her think about another

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:30.100
option is not to try to talk him or her out
of his, their beliefs, you know?

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:33.920
Don't think that, that's not the way your,
you know, your faith really wants that never

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:34.920
works.

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:40.180
But what we usually do is work to build relationships,
build true engagement on other kinds of, you

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:41.670
know, what we call plurality, right.

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:46.850
So, so thinking about the world as a world
that's better with multiple points of view

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:51.700
by showing it and living it with the kid,
with um, you know, by showing them that, hey,

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:56.420
I'm a, I'm a Brooklyn, New York, you know,
mom of three kids and I really care about

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:59.500
hearing about your ideas and your thoughts,
let's engage.

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:00.500
That.

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:05.690
That's sort of more a way towards, I think,
breaking that, the ingroup rigidity too, you

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:06.690
know.

00:27:06.690 --> 00:27:07.690
So it's interesting parallel.

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:08.690
Yeah.

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:12.500
I wonder if we can segue, do we have an image
of the two echo chambers online from the.

00:27:12.500 --> 00:27:17.890
So this is a study that I ran with Billy Brady
and Julian Wills and John Jost in my lab here

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:18.890
at NYU.

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:25.630
We analyzed over a half a million tweets of
regular people discussing hot button political

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:29.551
issues like the ones we're talking about here,
same sex marriage, climate change, um, gun

00:27:29.551 --> 00:27:31.630
control, online.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:38.700
And what we found is when they were using
moral emotional words like anger, hate, disgust,

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:43.980
in their tweets for every additional moral,
emotional word in a tweet, it was about 15

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:46.150
to 20 percent more likely to be retweeted.

00:27:46.150 --> 00:27:51.610
So that's how things get contagious online,
at least what we found in our data.

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:55.330
And the other thing though, so that's a positive,
if you ever want to have a successful social

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:57.900
media campaign for your next book?

00:27:57.900 --> 00:28:02.650
You can find some moral, emotional language
about the poker table.

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:03.950
Excellent.

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:08.340
So that spreads information that's more likely
to be reshared by others, but we can figure

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:12.130
out what your political leaning is based on
who you follow and who follows you.

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:15.610
So if you follow Barack Obama and Hillary
Clinton, we can infer that you're probably

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:16.610
pretty liberal.

00:28:16.610 --> 00:28:19.790
You Follow Donald Trump and George Bush, you're
more likely to be conservative.

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:23.670
And so you can plot people based on their
political beliefs and what you find is when

00:28:23.670 --> 00:28:29.450
they're using this moral emotional language,
this is what you see is that it's going viral,

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:35.940
but only within these echo chambers and so
you get people just talking to themselves

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:40.580
and you get what looks like a division and
so we don't know if this is the moral emotional

00:28:40.580 --> 00:28:42.810
language is causing this or or what.

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:47.320
That's one thing you don't get from social
media, but it's kind of the exact pathology

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:48.600
that we're talking about.

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:53.870
And so in another study I ran with Mina Cikara,
she was the first author.

00:28:53.870 --> 00:28:58.740
We actually showed people social networks
that looked like the one you just saw and

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:02.060
then measured their empathy for ingroup members
and outgroup members.

00:29:02.060 --> 00:29:06.610
And if they saw that the two groups look like
that, they suddenly showed very little empathy

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:08.000
for the outgroup.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:12.000
If you show them a social network that looks
integrated and overlapping, their empathy

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:15.350
suddenly increases for the outgroup.

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:16.660
And this was not in a political context.

00:29:16.660 --> 00:29:21.490
This is with flipping a coin and creating
two teams, but I feel kind of bad because

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:25.210
that image that I just showed you actually
went viral when we published this paper, but

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:28.670
if anything it perpetuates the problem because
people start to see the groups as more divided

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:34.570
than they than they maybe really are, and
that it makes them less likely to empathize

00:29:34.570 --> 00:29:37.200
with the other groups because they start to
think we're not connected in any way.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:42.650
We don't have shared social networks or friends
and so it's kind of we're in, maybe this is

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:43.910
why Jon thinks it's getting worse.

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:49.360
I'd love to be in a virtuous cycle, but we're
in a negative spiral and it's just mutually

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:50.360
reinforcing.

00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:51.360
No, that's right.

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:56.550
I think this is a really nice connection back
to the point about, uh, about process.

00:29:56.550 --> 00:30:01.040
If you think about a democratic society and
this amazing thing we've done of building

00:30:01.040 --> 00:30:05.300
an ethnically and religiously and racially
diverse democratic society, that was the envy

00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:11.140
of the world for much of the 20th century,
and you have this really complex system and

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:15.010
then you reach in, you change a couple of
the parameters, so it's sort of like imagine

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:20.060
like the whole universe is held together and
there's 25 constants in physics, and imagine

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:23.451
if you reached in and you suddenly just like
you took the gravitational constant and you

00:30:23.451 --> 00:30:27.310
just multiplied it by a thousand, like what
would happen to the universe, like, I don't

00:30:27.310 --> 00:30:28.630
know, let's find out?

00:30:28.630 --> 00:30:32.210
Well, what would happen if you...what if we
connected everybody?

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:33.210
Let's connect everybody.

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:38.170
Say everyone can talk to everyone and we'll
just multiply, increase the constant of relatance

00:30:38.170 --> 00:30:39.280
by a thousandfold.

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:40.510
What could, what could go wrong?

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:41.510
I don't know.

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:44.790
Let's find out...lots can go right.

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:46.330
So like Facebook.

00:30:46.330 --> 00:30:49.920
I mean they're very idealistic and there's
a lot of good that happened, so I think.

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:52.790
I think they didn't because they had this
very optimistic view of what if we just, you

00:30:52.790 --> 00:30:56.150
know, connect everybody and if only there
was no, you know, only there were no nations,

00:30:56.150 --> 00:31:00.310
no religions too, fill it all the people...You
know, so.

00:31:00.310 --> 00:31:05.850
But yeah, I think that because human nature
is prone to this violent tribalism, I think

00:31:05.850 --> 00:31:08.280
social media on net has been a huge negative.

00:31:08.280 --> 00:31:09.280
Okay.

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:10.280
We don't know.

00:31:10.280 --> 00:31:11.650
I'm not confident in that judgment, but I
fear that it has been a huge...

00:31:11.650 --> 00:31:16.210
So it just amplifies because I, this is what
I hear all the time when I'm talking to these

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:22.330
kids and they're telling me how they became
interested and you know, radicalized essentially.

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:24.070
And it's just so sad.

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:28.330
I mean because what they talk about is sitting
in their room and these are kids with emotional

00:31:28.330 --> 00:31:29.330
problems.

00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:31.590
They've, you know, they have usually, as I
said, had childhood trauma.

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:35.371
They have trouble with empathy, they have
trouble feeling good about themselves, and

00:31:35.371 --> 00:31:41.690
so they sit in their room and they have a
constant feed of language that enhances their

00:31:41.690 --> 00:31:48.090
sense of grievance and that the failures that
they have are related to a larger mission

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:54.570
and they are alienated then from a, from a
group that might say, yes, there's grievance,

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:58.120
yes, there's things that have been wrong,
but let's, let's do something to fight.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:31:59.300
Let's do something to fix it.

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:03.020
Let's, you know, they get alienated from that
and they become, you know, echo chambered

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:10.630
into this very, very malignant language and
it's an ultimately it fuels a lot of what

00:32:10.630 --> 00:32:14.440
they decide to do and it's really...and then
they actually have contact with real people.

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:19.830
It's not just websites and videos which are
very, very disturbing, but there's people

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:22.880
out there who will communicate and say, yeah,
yeah, do stuff.

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:23.880
That's right.

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:27.240
So I think we're probably as psychologists
I think we'll all agree that a really important

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:31.491
thing in everyone's life and especially adolescents
is prestige, is what people think of you and

00:32:31.491 --> 00:32:32.830
your peer group.

00:32:32.830 --> 00:32:37.610
And there's a really useful concept to quote
an externality in a, you know, economists

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:41.480
talking externalities where, you know, if
I buy something but I don't pay the full cost,

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:43.820
I'm actually, every time I buy it I'm actually
imposing costs on others.

00:32:43.820 --> 00:32:50.690
And so pollution is like that...Well, social
media has set up vast, vast externalities

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:56.410
in which, in my group I want to get prestige
and I can get prestige by saying hateful things

00:32:56.410 --> 00:32:57.550
about her.

00:32:57.550 --> 00:33:01.160
And I don't know her, I don't care about her,
but let's just all attack her and whoever

00:33:01.160 --> 00:33:05.780
can attack her most viciously, most creatively
with the most nasty names, that person gets

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:08.910
more prestige and none of us care what happens
to her.

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:12.360
And so this I think is what most alarms me
about social media, is that it used to be

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:16.841
that a lot of our conversations were to the
person that we were talking to, but a whole

00:33:16.841 --> 00:33:20.160
generation is being raised online where they're
not really talking to each other.

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:24.730
They're talking to display to others and their
talk has all kinds of bad effects, but they're

00:33:24.730 --> 00:33:25.860
not responsible for that.

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:30.000
So there's a gigantic market failure in this
sort of the social communication market.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:33.770
One thing I'll tell teenagers who I work with
is including my own children, is if you're

00:33:33.770 --> 00:33:38.110
having a strong emotion about a person or
about yourself and you're looking at a screen,

00:33:38.110 --> 00:33:41.100
you need to get up and walk away from the
screen and deal with another human.

00:33:41.100 --> 00:33:45.220
Because a strong emotion and screens are rarely
a good combination.

00:33:45.220 --> 00:33:46.970
I'm not talking about watching a movie and
you feel something.

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:52.610
I'm talking about you're feeling rage or you're
feeling humiliated and small and you just

00:33:52.610 --> 00:33:53.610
keep at it.

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:54.800
You stay in that loop.

00:33:54.800 --> 00:33:55.800
That's never a good thing.

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:56.800
Especially for teenagers.

00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:57.800
They're not developed.

00:33:57.800 --> 00:34:02.460
Their frontal lobe is not there yet in the,
hmm, maybe this is not the worst, you know,

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:05.220
the worst moment of my life and I should go
do, you know, maybe there's actually going

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:06.350
to be some improvement here.

00:34:06.350 --> 00:34:10.159
Now my daughter is eight, and last month it
was a crazy thing happened.

00:34:10.159 --> 00:34:13.101
She was sitting on the sofa and she said,
Daddy, can you come take my iPad away?

00:34:13.101 --> 00:34:16.139
I can't take my eyes off it.

00:34:16.139 --> 00:34:18.100
That's really sad.

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:22.100
So this actually reminds me of some of the
work you've done, Jay, on depersonalization

00:34:22.100 --> 00:34:23.100
and outgroups.

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:27.750
Um, can you talk a little bit about that and
about how kind of those two areas of your

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:30.850
research might actually end up overlapping
and amplifying each other?

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:32.000
Yeah, so thanks for yeah.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:36.530
So one thing, as I'm hearing these conversations,
one reason I love to have these debates in

00:34:36.530 --> 00:34:41.790
person on a panel like this is because you
can contextualize what you're saying with

00:34:41.790 --> 00:34:46.450
richer language, but also with your body language
and smiling and nodding and so you can agree

00:34:46.450 --> 00:34:50.600
with somebody in as we've been doing in a
comfortable way that you move the conversation

00:34:50.600 --> 00:34:55.490
forward and you identify where you share reality
and where you disagree.

00:34:55.490 --> 00:35:00.130
Whereas online I hate debating people on Twitter
and I'm on Twitter a lot and I like sharing

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:02.210
stuff and having scientific discussion.

00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:03.450
And you're very gracious.

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:04.450
You should follow him.

00:35:04.450 --> 00:35:06.370
What is it...Jay Van Bave...what is it?

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:07.600
Yeah, Jay Van Bavel.

00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:09.580
He's, he's a great science tweeter.

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:14.190
But I will not debate people on Twitter, and
here's why.

00:35:14.190 --> 00:35:15.820
Because you lose all the context.

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:19.570
There's not enough characters to add nuance
and to say, well, I agree with you on this

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:21.250
part, Jon, but I disagree here.

00:35:21.250 --> 00:35:22.780
There's just not the space for it.

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:28.560
The other thing is it's happening in public
and so reputation management and status is

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:32.950
on the line in a way that is especially in
view if you think of thousands of people or

00:35:32.950 --> 00:35:37.590
if something gets taken out of context and
retweeted by a lot of people, it's horrifying

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:39.380
if it's happened, you and it's happened to
me.

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:43.640
I'm sure that's happened to you and I've had
lots of friends and they're just horrified

00:35:43.640 --> 00:35:48.940
and they want to unplug or they shut down
their account after and it's the worst possible

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:51.180
context and you also don't get the human emotion.

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:55.580
You can't see their face and see if they're
joking or being sarcastic or trying to attack

00:35:55.580 --> 00:35:56.580
you.

00:35:56.580 --> 00:35:59.770
Whereas you can sense that in person and bridge
the gap much easier.

00:35:59.770 --> 00:36:04.560
And so as we've moved our human interactions
around hot button issues like politics and

00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:09.800
morality online, you've gotten rid of all
the things that help us deescalate conflict

00:36:09.800 --> 00:36:13.120
and have civility or most of them I think.

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:17.110
Um, and then the other thing I want to speak
to, and this is my main concern with social

00:36:17.110 --> 00:36:20.840
media is, um, if you go to the Wall Street
Journal, they have this great page called

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:22.320
red feed blue feed.

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:26.660
You can pick a topic like immigration or gun
control and it will show you what's being

00:36:26.660 --> 00:36:30.820
seen by somebody on the extreme left or the
extreme right in this country.

00:36:30.820 --> 00:36:35.070
And it's horrific the feeds and how different
they are, but also the types of news that

00:36:35.070 --> 00:36:36.930
are drawing from are not the Associated Press.

00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:39.760
Um, it's from obscure news sites that are
hyperpartisan.

00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:45.080
And, and part of it is because there's an
incentive structure built into the money that

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:49.260
social media companies make, which is you
get a lot of clicks for things that are extreme

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:51.530
and people are more glued to their screen.

00:36:51.530 --> 00:36:53.490
Like your poor child.

00:36:53.490 --> 00:36:58.610
If you feed them back stuff that fits with
their belief system, ideology, and so my hope

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:02.190
actually, and I think that this is, there's
more research going on and I'm hoping that

00:37:02.190 --> 00:37:07.960
they find a solution is for companies like
Facebook to update their algorithms in ways

00:37:07.960 --> 00:37:15.930
that feed people quality information and deescalate
the partitioning of society into these extreme

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:16.930
chambers.

00:37:16.930 --> 00:37:21.040
Yeah that worked really well for Pepsi when
they tried to create healthier foods.

00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:22.580
Didn't last long.

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:24.630
They had to stop.

00:37:24.630 --> 00:37:26.170
I'm an optimist.

00:37:26.170 --> 00:37:27.170
So.

00:37:27.170 --> 00:37:32.200
Well I was also thinking about your work,
um, which, which is very scary to me that

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:37.100
when, we see someone as an outgroup member,
we don't see them as having a mind.

00:37:37.100 --> 00:37:42.370
And could you talk a little bit about how
we actually, and because I'm what I'm worried

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:45.320
about hearing what you guys are saying about
social media, is that this might actually

00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:50.200
amplify some of those propensities to not
see the other as even human.

00:37:50.200 --> 00:37:51.200
Okay.

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:52.810
So do we have the video or the image for this?

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:55.450
Of the morphing between a human and a doll?

00:37:55.450 --> 00:37:59.480
So this was a study that I ran in my lab,
Leor Hackel was the first author and we got

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:04.360
all these great morphs from Christine Looser,
a colleague of ours, and she had found dolls

00:38:04.360 --> 00:38:08.920
that look like human and match them up and
then use computer software to morph them across

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:13.870
the spectrum from 100 percent human to zero
percent human, 100 percent doll.

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:17.630
And you could go from 100 percent human to
90 percent human, 80 percent human along the

00:38:17.630 --> 00:38:18.810
spectrum.

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:22.360
And what we found is that the moment people
are part of a team or a group, again, you

00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:28.160
can flip a coin and create groups that the
point at which they start to see a face look

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:30.080
human takes more humanness.

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:34.160
And so if it's an out group member, you start
to see the person looking human around 60

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:36.750
percent humanness.

00:38:36.750 --> 00:38:39.550
But if they're an in group member, it's like
55 percent.

00:38:39.550 --> 00:38:43.511
So in other words, it takes less humanness
to see somebody as having a mind if they're

00:38:43.511 --> 00:38:44.511
an in group member.

00:38:44.511 --> 00:38:48.310
And we found this also with NYU students,
and this is really only among the students

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:49.390
who are highly identified.

00:38:49.390 --> 00:38:52.910
So if they don't care about their group and
at NYU you have some students who could care

00:38:52.910 --> 00:38:53.910
less about NYU.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:55.850
They're here because they want to be in New
York.

00:38:55.850 --> 00:39:00.090
So for them they don't show this effect, but
the moment you start to identify your group,

00:39:00.090 --> 00:39:02.590
the fact actually looks quite big.

00:39:02.590 --> 00:39:07.220
And so what we think is happening is part
of this broader literature on dehumanization,

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:10.340
which has been in the news a lot lately about
how we talk about other groups.

00:39:10.340 --> 00:39:18.590
If you don't see another group as having a,
you know, experience or emotions, that's tends

00:39:18.590 --> 00:39:24.280
to be if you look at really horrible experiences
in human history like the genocide in Rwanda

00:39:24.280 --> 00:39:29.520
or Nazi Germany, what you see as propaganda
that tells you this group is, is a rats or

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:31.590
this group is cockroaches.

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:36.230
And what that is doing is dehumanizing people
and that allows you to harm somebody.

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:40.700
And this goes to a point I just want to bridge,
you made earlier, Kate, which is that a lot

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:42.670
of people, we flip a coin here, we can create
groups.

00:39:42.670 --> 00:39:44.770
There's not gonna be any violence.

00:39:44.770 --> 00:39:45.920
You're totally right.

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:46.920
Violence is really hard.

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:51.130
And so in World War One, when they looked
at the weapons and trench warfare that were

00:39:51.130 --> 00:39:53.610
fired, very few people actually fired their
gun.

00:39:53.610 --> 00:39:56.760
They stood there over the trench and made
it look like they were shooting, but they

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:59.140
were staring at somebody across the trenches.

00:39:59.140 --> 00:40:03.230
And when you see somebody's face, it's very
hard for normal humans to pull the trigger

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:04.230
and harm somebody.

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:09.200
So our military has got really good at training
people to shoot to kill people.

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:13.000
But it took a lot of work because it does
not come naturally to most people.

00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:17.310
And so if you have some existing pathology
or background of trauma, you're going to be

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:23.230
the subset of people who are likely to use
these cues to then do something bad.

00:40:23.230 --> 00:40:27.180
But this is part of when you see propaganda
and these things, it's setting the stage,

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:31.510
if you start to dehumanize a group to allow
you to be more comfortable harming them.

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:32.510
Yeah, yeah.

00:40:32.510 --> 00:40:38.850
I mean the, the other psychological concept
that links up for me regarding the kids and

00:40:38.850 --> 00:40:45.110
young people I've worked with who tended to
make these terrible decisions is a sense of

00:40:45.110 --> 00:40:48.780
what we call, in in clinical psych we call
emotional dysregulation and that is sort of

00:40:48.780 --> 00:40:52.960
not being able to deal with your feelings,
not being able to recognize them, not being

00:40:52.960 --> 00:40:56.950
able to name them not being able to process
them, not being able to then recognize them

00:40:56.950 --> 00:40:57.950
in others.

00:40:57.950 --> 00:41:01.870
So now obviously we're, you know, the emotional
dysregulation leads very clearly into empathy

00:41:01.870 --> 00:41:02.920
issues.

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:10.510
And so the idea of kids who are raised in
a, if you raise a kid, you know, abusing them,

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:14.920
humiliating them, I mean, I'm talking about
kids who've watched their fathers just viciously

00:41:14.920 --> 00:41:16.910
brutalize their mothers in the home.

00:41:16.910 --> 00:41:19.880
Um, and this little kid is cowering literally
in the corner.

00:41:19.880 --> 00:41:24.280
So he's, he's being traumatized by the most,
the closest people in his life, his parents,

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:25.280
especially the father.

00:41:25.280 --> 00:41:30.720
You know, that kid develops an emotional regulation
problem, most likely, I mean, the, the, the,

00:41:30.720 --> 00:41:35.350
the couple of kids I'm thinking of as I put
that example together, um, you know, what

00:41:35.350 --> 00:41:40.060
they develop, what he develops is an inability
to handle his feelings and therefore feelings

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.430
become very, very overwhelming and frightening
to him.

00:41:42.430 --> 00:41:46.230
And so when he has a feeling he's going to
be less likely to be able to deal with it.

00:41:46.230 --> 00:41:50.770
And so then again, put him in that echo chamber
where where the kinds of things that are being

00:41:50.770 --> 00:41:56.810
stoked are anger and rage and you know, we're
being humiliated, you know, us white people,

00:41:56.810 --> 00:42:01.119
us, whoever, fill in the blanks and you have
