00:46:30.640 --> 00:46:35.890
were to be any sort of, of a nuclear exchange
or even just the grid going down if things

00:46:35.890 --> 00:46:36.890
got really bad.

00:46:36.890 --> 00:46:40.860
There are a lot of ways in which things might
be more fragile than we think.

00:46:40.860 --> 00:46:41.860
Yeah.

00:46:41.860 --> 00:46:43.420
Kate, what about you from an individual level?

00:46:43.420 --> 00:46:46.540
I'm, I'm pretty darn pessimistic.

00:46:46.540 --> 00:46:51.170
Sorry, I think we're going to move our negative
chairs over here.

00:46:51.170 --> 00:46:54.690
But my problem, I mean, I, I really toil in
the world of human cruelty.

00:46:54.690 --> 00:46:59.350
I mean that's kind of what I do and because
I work at a clinic for people who are fleeing

00:46:59.350 --> 00:47:02.100
war zones and fleeing situations of frank
torture.

00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:08.700
And so I spent a lot of time looking at the
cost of, of really incredible dehumanization

00:47:08.700 --> 00:47:10.300
and cruelty.

00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:12.970
Let me say one optimistic thing, so they don't
all go running, screaming out of the room.

00:47:12.970 --> 00:47:16.310
I mean, I, I have great faith in us as beings.

00:47:16.310 --> 00:47:17.470
I think humans are amazing.

00:47:17.470 --> 00:47:19.360
I also think that that.

00:47:19.360 --> 00:47:26.170
I actually think that recovery from a virulent
extremism and virulent hate is possible.

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:27.170
I mean, I've seen it.

00:47:27.170 --> 00:47:31.930
I've worked with guys who have turned it around
