00:50:22.090 --> 00:50:23.090
who get a lot of attention...

00:50:23.090 --> 00:50:24.090
...I definitely have that problem...

00:50:24.090 --> 00:50:28.930
So those are the people you see at your clinic
and those are the people that Jon sees, who

00:50:28.930 --> 00:50:32.830
respond to you in social media or we observe
talking about.

00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:37.550
We're constantly holding up people who have
the most extreme attitudes rather than looking

00:50:37.550 --> 00:50:42.670
at where the median is and what the consensus
is and what the distribution is.

00:50:42.670 --> 00:50:47.880
We don't see the people in the middle, even
though that's 60 to 70 percent of the population,

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:51.940
what we see are the people on the extremes
making the most noise or engaging in the most

00:50:51.940 --> 00:50:52.980
outrageous behavior.

00:50:52.980 --> 00:50:56.420
And we point to them as examples of what the
other side is all like.

00:50:56.420 --> 00:50:59.540
But the other side is not actually that homogenous.

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:09.030
And so I do believe that we might have a misperception
of what the consensus belief is on a lot of

00:51:09.030 --> 00:51:11.470
these types of issues that are divisive.

00:51:11.470 --> 00:51:17.230
Um, and so I end up finding the data from
Pinker fairly compelling that we're on a good

00:51:17.230 --> 00:51:19.450
