with the World War Two veterans, do you think,

01:13:06.010 --> 01:13:10.380
uh, the military, do you think we trained
the military to become extremists?

01:13:10.380 --> 01:13:12.150
And do you think that's moral?

01:13:12.150 --> 01:13:16.300
No, I don't think we trained the military
to become extremists.

01:13:16.300 --> 01:13:18.300
I think that there.

01:13:18.300 --> 01:13:21.749
I've spoken a couple times at West Point,
that's the service that I know best.

01:13:21.749 --> 01:13:26.119
There's, there's a really intense moral culture
of service and honor.

01:13:26.119 --> 01:13:28.199
That doesn't mean that there's not also cheating
scandals.

01:13:28.199 --> 01:13:29.670
They're human.

01:13:29.670 --> 01:13:33.079
But I think if you look at, if you look at
soldiers and think, wow, they kill people,

01:13:33.079 --> 01:13:34.119
they must be extremists.

01:13:34.119 --> 01:13:37.749
I think that really misunderstands what it's
about from the inside.

01:13:37.749 --> 01:13:42.459
So no, I think the military is definitely
a way of taking people who are very diverse

01:13:42.459 --> 01:13:47.610
psychologically and in terms of identities
and giving them an overarching identity, giving

01:13:47.610 --> 01:13:50.139
them a sense of one for all, all for one.

01:13:50.139 --> 01:13:52.020
And I think the military is interesting.

01:13:52.020 --> 01:13:56.070
It should be especially interesting to us
now because A, it's one of the only institutions

01:13:56.070 --> 01:14:01.579
for which there is still high regard and B
or two, whatever I said the first time.

01:14:01.579 --> 01:14:07.590
Secondly is it's one of the most successful
at, uh, at addressing racial problems and

01:14:07.590 --> 01:14:08.659
disparities.

01:14:08.659 --> 01:14:11.340
So I think they've been much more successful
in the academic world.

01:14:11.340 --> 01:14:15.289
And there's a wonderful book by two military
sociologists called All That We Can Be, where

01:14:15.289 --> 01:14:20.239
they explain how the military did it because
they had terrible racism up through the seventies.

01:14:20.239 --> 01:14:22.869
And once they realized, wow, this is a serious
command problem.

01:14:22.869 --> 01:14:27.039
This is a serious readiness or operational
problem that are, that were divided by race

01:14:27.039 --> 01:14:31.320
and racism and they really addressed in an
intelligent way and they specifically contrast

01:14:31.320 --> 01:14:33.210
what they did with what we do with the universities.

01:14:33.210 --> 01:14:35.360
And thank God we didn't do it that way.

01:14:35.360 --> 01:14:41.079
If I may say one thing, I was in the army
and I recall during basic training one time

01:14:41.079 --> 01:14:45.340
where you had to put your rifle over, your
head and you had to shout, kill, kill, kill.

01:14:45.340 --> 01:14:47.739
And I didn't want to kill anybody because
I didn't hit anybody.

01:14:47.739 --> 01:14:49.320
And so did others.

01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:52.489
And they choose people based upon how they
responded to that.

01:14:52.489 --> 01:14:55.420
Those who did not were not sent to combat.

01:14:55.420 --> 01:15:00.409
But the solidarity that Jon talked about is
I saw the same thing with the Taliban.

01:15:00.409 --> 01:15:05.889
And the same thing with the Mujahideen, um,
that you found that I found in the military

01:15:05.889 --> 01:15:07.050
group solidarity.

01:15:07.050 --> 01:15:10.119
And the other is the enemy.

01:15:10.119 --> 01:15:14.580
During, in Vietnam era, American soldiers
called them gooks and slant heads.

01:15:14.580 --> 01:15:20.469
And in Afghanistan I noticed they called them
hajis and ragheads.

01:15:20.469 --> 01:15:26.610
Um, so there was this sense that the united,
that the military had to differentiate them.

01:15:26.610 --> 01:15:30.530
Differentiate themselves from people who were
once our very close allies in one way they

01:15:30.530 --> 01:15:36.900
did it inadvertently accidentally was by same
thing that soldiers did during my era, in

01:15:36.900 --> 01:15:37.900
Vietnam...

01:15:37.900 --> 01:15:41.110
But did the training encourage them to see
them as subhuman or is that something that

01:15:41.110 --> 01:15:42.619
emerges as people are in combat?

01:15:42.619 --> 01:15:43.960
Oh I'm convinced it emerged.

01:15:43.960 --> 01:15:45.320
Yeah, it merged.

01:15:45.320 --> 01:15:46.320
Yeah.

01:15:46.320 --> 01:15:47.320
Thank you.

01:15:47.320 --> 01:15:48.320
We can do one last question

01:15:48.320 --> 01:15:53.360
Someone said early on that this group, we
probably won't examine our own extremism,

01:15:53.360 --> 01:15:56.699
our own sides, and I found that to be tremendously
important that when I look at when, when I

01:15:56.699 --> 01:15:57.699
talk about the behaviors that you guys are
talking about, the patterns that you're talking

01:15:57.699 --> 01:15:58.699
about..

01:15:58.699 --> 01:15:59.699
Is that something that people are working
on, I mean our own ingroup?

01:15:59.699 --> 01:16:00.699
Is that something people are working on?

01:16:00.699 --> 01:16:05.440
Is that something we didn't, you guys said
we wouldn't do it and we didn't.

01:16:05.440 --> 01:16:08.059
Yeah, I mean I'll, I'll say two things.

01:16:08.059 --> 01:16:14.630
One is, um, I have a close collaborator who
studies things like dissent and groupthink

01:16:14.630 --> 01:16:21.070
and the people who are willing to speak up
within their own group when it's going wrong...are

01:16:21.070 --> 01:16:24.619
the people who identify actually most of the
group and they think they can see that it's

01:16:24.619 --> 01:16:27.429
heading in a direction that it's going to
be damaging to the group in the long run.

01:16:27.429 --> 01:16:29.590
There was a study that came out on this last
week.

01:16:29.590 --> 01:16:35.750
It was covered in the Washington Post that
people treat ingroup dissenters just as bad

01:16:35.750 --> 01:16:37.159
as outgroup members.

01:16:37.159 --> 01:16:39.850
And this study was done in the political domain.

01:16:39.850 --> 01:16:45.550
Um, and this is why, so you referenced, you
alluded to John McCain, you know, when he

01:16:45.550 --> 01:16:49.979
spoke out against, got in conflict with Donald
Trump or pop, a lot of republicans treated

01:16:49.979 --> 01:16:55.809
him very badly and his approval plummeted,
same with Jeff Flake in Arizona and this is

01:16:55.809 --> 01:17:02.630
a problem that is a deeper psychological problem,
which is that we have to think carefully about

01:17:02.630 --> 01:17:09.699
embracing constructive ingroup dissenters
more wholly to avoid from going astray and

01:17:09.699 --> 01:17:14.349
whatever level we define our identity at,
whether that's at the national level or at

01:17:14.349 --> 01:17:16.079
the university level or political party level.

01:17:16.079 --> 01:17:19.769
So I think that self examination is a critical
part of it, but it turns out it's really hard.

01:17:19.769 --> 01:17:22.800
We don't treat people who are self examining
our group very well.

01:17:22.800 --> 01:17:24.320
Thank you.

01:17:24.320 --> 01:17:29.610
Well, on that note, I would like to just say
thank you to Jay, to Kate, to Jonathan, to

01:17:29.610 --> 01:17:30.610
Jere.

01:17:30.610 --> 01:17:34.440
This has been one of the most interesting
panels I've had the privilege of ever moderating,

01:17:34.440 --> 01:17:45.110
so thank you guys so much.

